00:06:02.545 --> 00:06:04.760
is a really interesting
use case for that.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:09.350
We're seeing companies go in
and have more immersive training

00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:11.960
courses, particularly for field
workers where they've actually

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:15.500
got to experience some of the
situations they might find

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.570
themselves in, and
virtual reality

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:20.000
could be a really
interesting opportunity

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:21.470
within the enterprise.

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:23.300
And similarly,
augmented reality when

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:26.390
you're actually in a sort
of field worker scenario

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:29.120
and you need help,
being able to overlay

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:30.980
that against your field
of view rather than

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:33.680
having to look up in a
text document on a tablet,

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.760
for example.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.680
And then finally, IoT
is an emerging area.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:41.270
There's obviously a lot of
presentations here this week

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:44.900
about IoT and how Google Cloud
is getting into that area,

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:50.450
and Android, with the recent
GA launch of Android Things,

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:53.550
is also getting
present in there too.

00:06:56.170 --> 00:06:58.330
One of the challenges
we've heard

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:02.620
about the broad range of
devices, though, is that, well,

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:07.620
how do I choose the devices
that I want in my enterprise?

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:11.740
There were over 400 different
manufacturers developing

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:15.460
devices with running
Android, and so navigating

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:18.620
that ecosystem is
really very difficult.

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:20.500
So what we did earlier
this year was we

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:24.370
launched a program called
Android Enterprise Recommended.

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:31.020
What this does is it creates
a higher level of requirements

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:35.290
around things like security,
device capabilities,

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:40.240
and consistency of the user
experience across devices

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.410
to make sure that
its easier for IT

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:49.060
decision-makers to decide which
devices to purchase and then

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:51.100
be confident that
those devices have

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:55.210
the longevity and the simplicity
required of an enterprise

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:57.680
environment.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:01.330
So a lot of that is a
focus is on selection,

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:04.780
but particularly a point which
has been painful in the Android

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:07.120
ecosystem is around updates.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.550
And so what we do with
Android Enterprise Recommended

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:11.920
is that those
devices are required

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:16.000
to have 90-day security
patch updates for three years

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:18.250
after the release
of that device.

00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:21.370
And they also have to have
at least one dessert release

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:22.940
update as well.

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:26.860
So that's starting to sort
of push the Android ecosystem

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:33.059
into a more updated mode.

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:37.250
We've already got now
over 40 devices from nine

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:39.890
different manufacturers,
and this just

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:42.500
gives us some examples
of some of the devices

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:43.941
that already ranged.

00:08:43.941 --> 00:08:45.440
Now these, you'll
see that these are

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:48.480
all kind of high-end
knowledge work devices.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.990
What we're doing now is
we're working with the OEMs

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:55.750
to also come down the
range of price points

00:08:55.750 --> 00:09:03.600
to have a wide range of mid-tier
and lower tier devices as well.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:05.700
What we're starting
to hear from customers

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:07.920
who are aware of the Android
Enterprise Recommended

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:08.600
program--

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:12.080
sorry, I'll try and get out
of the way so you can see--

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:18.630
is that the list of devices
that we've approved through AER

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:20.700
is actually helping
customers now

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.490
select not only the
kinds of devices

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:25.230
that they want to
purchase themselves

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:29.820
as corporate-liable devices,
but also the set of devices

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:32.490
that they're willing to
support as part of a bring

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:34.740
your own device program.

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:39.240
So this was a survey that
we sent out to our customers

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:40.930
just recently.

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:46.590
And really starting to show a
high degree of traction there.

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:49.830
How are we evolving Android
Enterprise recommended?

00:09:49.830 --> 00:09:51.660
Well, we're bringing
in new device types.

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:55.702
So we've got a rugged device
program coming in soon, so

00:09:55.702 --> 00:09:57.660
a slightly different set
of requirements there.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:01.890
Things like drop test
requirements coming in.

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:05.340
We're also looking at applying
the principles of Android

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:08.490
Enterprise Recommended to
different partner types,

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:15.360
to the EMM and UEM type vendors,
systems integrators as well,

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:16.890
and they're also
developing programs

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:20.440
for carriers and
developers too so

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:25.110
we can give a degree of
confidence to our customers

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:31.179
that these products and services
have reached a higher bar.

00:10:31.179 --> 00:10:32.970
So my colleague Ken
[? Shutt ?] is actually

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:35.180
going to be talking
in the next slot

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:39.570
after this over
the way in Moscone

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:42.490
West in much, much more detail
about all these programs.

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:48.790
So if you want to hear more,
do catch his session later on.

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:50.380
So let's change
gears now and talk

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:57.070
about the comprehensiveness of
Android's management framework.

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:59.320
And the way I want
to start here is

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:03.190
with the range of different
modes of operation

00:11:03.190 --> 00:11:05.740
that we have for
Android devices.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:08.020
So starting on the
left hand side there,

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:10.510
for bring your own device,
we have this concept

00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:13.320
called a work profile.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:16.870
What a work profile does is
it's a separate user, really,

00:11:16.870 --> 00:11:21.550
in the Android operating
system, but it's the apps

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:23.980
and the data are shown
to users at the same time

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:26.110
in the launcher,
and they're badged

00:11:26.110 --> 00:11:30.970
with the little briefcase
icon there so users

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:33.120
can see whether
they're using the work

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:34.530
app or the personal app.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:36.910
And you can navigate
between these apps

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:41.520
just as you would any
other two apps personally.

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:45.670
But the benefits are that you
can set policies around how

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:47.900
enterprise data can be shared.

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:50.230
You can switch off
things like copy paste,

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:56.480
or sharing between apps through
the share sheet on apps.

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.500
But also a benefit for the user.

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:01.310
The user can be confident
that their personal usage

00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:05.660
of their own device isn't
being monitored by their IT

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:09.210
department, and should
they leave the company,

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:11.390
The only thing that the
IT department can delete

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:14.360
is the corporate data,
the work apps and data,

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:16.850
leaving all of their
personal apps and data

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:19.140
entirely untouched.

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:21.830
So benefit actually
for both sides.

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:24.680
It's also now possible
to be able to switch off

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:27.860
the work profile, like on
an evening or a weekend

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:32.500
or when you are on vacation,
which just enhances

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:37.040
that capability to keep
work separate and out

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:39.740
of your personal life.

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:43.010
Then moving onto the
company-owned side of things,

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:45.920
we can also have your
fully managed device which

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:48.740
has a few more capabilities
around things like factory

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:51.620
reset protection, and
being able to control

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:54.280
what Wi-Fi hot spots a
device can attach to,

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:57.580
things are VPN as well.

00:12:57.580 --> 00:12:59.890
But also you can add a
work profile to that.

00:12:59.890 --> 00:13:03.244
So you still get the benefits
of the data separation,

00:13:03.244 --> 00:13:04.660
but if you're a
company that wants

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:08.670
to allow your users to use the
corporate devices personally

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:11.380
and have your employees only
carry around one device, which

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:15.010
is very convenient for
them, then you can certainly

00:13:15.010 --> 00:13:17.770
do that in a managed way.

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:21.340
Of course, you can have just
a solely fully managed device

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:23.530
which is corporate-only.

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:26.620
And then we've also got a mode
similar to the fully managed

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:29.680
device, which is what
we call dedicated.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:34.840
So this is where you can lock
a single or a very small number

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:39.760
of apps to a device, which use
access to things like settings

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:42.880
or recents or
notifications, even,

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:46.420
and so if you've got a device,
for example, for a logistics

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:49.540
worker who wants to focus
solely on delivering

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:53.440
packages or navigating to
their next drop-off point,

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:58.120
then you can do that without any
risk of the user accidentally

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:01.150
or intentionally changing
settings and causing

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:03.880
their device to no longer work.

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:08.170
So a comprehensive set
of different modes,

00:14:08.170 --> 00:14:09.730
depending on the
particular use case

00:14:09.730 --> 00:14:11.021
that you're trying to roll out.

00:14:13.670 --> 00:14:18.090
I want to highlight here just
the length of time and effort

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:22.260
that we've really spent on
building out the feature set

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:24.030
within Android Enterprise.

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:29.970
We started off in Lollipop
with the initial iteration

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:32.850
of the work profile and
the fully managed device,

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:36.152
and have continue to
evolve that over time

00:14:36.152 --> 00:14:40.890
through to Android Oreo,
which was, obviously, released

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:42.330
last fall.

00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:46.150
And then Android P that we've
got coming out fairly shortly.

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:50.070
I'll talk a little bit more
about that in a minute.

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:53.120
The investment that we've been
making over the four years

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:57.710
has really shone through
in terms of the adoption

00:14:57.710 --> 00:15:01.290
that we've seen from
enterprises globally, actually.

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:03.770
This is not just focused
on North America.

00:15:03.770 --> 00:15:08.930
We've also seen extensive
deployments in EMEA and JPAC.

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:10.700
And over the last
two years we've

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:13.670
consistently seen
10x growth in that.

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:17.990
So if you're considering now
rolling out Android Enterprise,

00:15:17.990 --> 00:15:20.600
you can be confident
that we're here to stay,

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:24.380
because this is really a
high-growth strategic project

00:15:24.380 --> 00:15:27.290
from Google.

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:30.150
So let's talk a bit more about
how do we build features in?

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:32.850
What are the principles
by which we are

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:35.550
evolving Android Enterprise?

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:40.800
First of all, we believe that
any modern operating system

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:44.550
should enable a user
to just carry around

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:48.580
a single device for work
and for personal, of course,

00:15:48.580 --> 00:15:51.780
if the IT policies allow that.

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:55.650
But if there are cases
where that isn't possible,

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:58.320
we still haven't quite got
our work profile right.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:01.440
So we will continue to
evolve until we can make sure

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:05.130
that every user who is today
carrying around two devices

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:08.220
can actually just
carry around one.

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:12.570
I already touched a little bit
on the one platform concept,

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:14.340
but it's really,
really crucial that we

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:17.580
allow a single management
capability across all

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:19.290
of those different
use cases, and we

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:22.880
will continue to strive
to make sure that happens.

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:24.730
And then the other
thing is ensuring

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:27.640
that app developers
don't have to build

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:31.180
the same app multiple times
for different container

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:33.020
technologies.

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:36.730
So the work profile is built
into the Android operating

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:39.400
system and will run any app.

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:42.340
There is no need for
any additional SDK

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:44.530
or any kind of app
wrapping that's

00:16:44.530 --> 00:16:47.620
historically been required.

00:16:47.620 --> 00:16:51.009
So let's talk a little bit
about a number of those things.

00:16:51.009 --> 00:16:52.800
And one of the challenges
that we've really

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:56.340
had for a long time now, since
we launched Android Enterprise,

00:16:56.340 --> 00:17:00.851
is, well, isn't this
just Samsung Knox?

00:17:00.851 --> 00:17:03.100
What's the difference between
Samsung Knox and Android

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:04.550
Enterprise?

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:07.599
And to date, it's
really been a choice

00:17:07.599 --> 00:17:11.640
for buyers of Samsung devices,
well, do you enroll into Knox

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:14.050
or do you enroll it
in Android Enterprise?

00:17:14.050 --> 00:17:18.700
Well, as of Android
Oreo and Knox 3.0,

00:17:18.700 --> 00:17:21.674
those two things are
now coming together.

00:17:21.674 --> 00:17:25.079
And now Samsung Knox,
the additional APIs

00:17:25.079 --> 00:17:27.490
and capabilities
that Knox brings

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:31.780
is built on top of the
basic Android Enterprise

00:17:31.780 --> 00:17:35.480
concepts of fully
managed and work profile.

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:38.110
So when you deploy
Knox you are actually

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:40.000
already deploying
Android Enterprise

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.190
with a extra bit on top.

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:44.290
So you no longer
have to make a choice

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:45.850
about what you are deploying.

00:17:45.850 --> 00:17:47.770
You could even start
with Android Enterprise

00:17:47.770 --> 00:17:52.880
and then upgrade to
Knox at a later date.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:56.190
So really exciting
convergence story there,

00:17:56.190 --> 00:18:00.670
and we continue to work
closely with Samsung to evolve

00:18:00.670 --> 00:18:02.660
that relationship further.

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:04.940
You can see more
information from Samsung

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:08.810
about the unification
just there.

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:10.320
Another thing
about one platform,

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:12.050
and if you saw the
previous session

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:16.100
where Mike [? Burr ?] was
hosting a number of partners

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:21.320
and customers just
before this, we've

00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:24.260
got an increasing
number of companies

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:27.150
working on dedicated devices.

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:29.660
So here are three
customer examples

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:34.400
of cases where they're
deploying, in some cases, quite

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:39.160
interesting hardware, and then
locking apps to that screen

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:42.830
to focus user
attention on the task

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:45.360
that that equipment
is designed for.

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:47.930
So on the left hand side
here, Marks and Spencer,

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:50.960
which is a very large
retailer in the UK,

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:58.420
deploying dedicated devices
to their shop floor assistants

00:18:58.420 --> 00:19:03.050
to really give them real time
information to help customers

00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:05.940
with their shopping.

00:19:05.940 --> 00:19:08.390
Pitney Bowes was
actually on stage,

00:19:08.390 --> 00:19:11.620
I saw, over the way
that just earlier,

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:15.110
who have got a famous
brand here in the US.

00:19:15.110 --> 00:19:18.180
They have a product
which is a postage meter.

00:19:18.180 --> 00:19:22.460
So you can print postage
labels and things

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:26.300
and pay for the postage
with the device.

00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:29.960
But now it's a connected product
actually running Android,

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:35.660
and that's enabled a wide
range of developers and apps

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:37.940
to come onto that platform
to actually extend

00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:40.340
the capabilities of a
product that they already

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:42.960
have within the office.

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:48.450
And then Chronopost is a
European logistics company

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:51.270
who have just rolled out
Android devices as well.

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:58.350
And you see by the numbers,
substantial volumes of devices

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:02.360
for all these kinds of sectors.

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:04.940
So I already
mentioned Android P,

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:08.890
and there's certainly
no let up on Android

00:20:08.890 --> 00:20:11.920
in terms of the investment
that we're putting in.

00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:16.760
And a couple of focus
areas I want to talk about,

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:20.410
we're focusing down on
more dedicated devices,

00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:23.410
particularly around things
like shift workers and shared

00:20:23.410 --> 00:20:24.900
devices.

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:29.380
So imagine you've got
your factory or warehouse

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:30.870
where you've got shift workers.

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:35.770
They sign out a device at
the beginning of their shift.

00:20:35.770 --> 00:20:38.620
They can log in and get
the apps and services

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:41.567
that they need as that
particular individual, right.

00:20:41.567 --> 00:20:43.150
They don't need to
worry about getting

00:20:43.150 --> 00:20:46.780
the right device for the apps.

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:50.570
The device will adapt to them
if they have different needs.

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:54.160
And we provide that now
natively in the platform.

00:20:54.160 --> 00:20:56.710
We're also introducing
a new-- well, slightly

00:20:56.710 --> 00:21:00.600
updated user experience
for the work profile

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:02.590
to really help users
better understand

00:21:02.590 --> 00:21:06.670
this concept of separation
between the work

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:08.170
and personal apps.

00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:12.100
We felt that the
ability to mix and match

00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:16.120
the apps across your launcher
in any which way is sometimes

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:17.860
a little
counter-intuitive to users

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:22.960
who don't understand correctly
how the data separation works.

00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:26.830
So we're refining that.

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:30.460
The other exciting thing about
Android P is that for the first

00:21:30.460 --> 00:21:34.620
time in Androids history, not
only have we launched the beta

00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:37.600
program for Google's own
hardware-- of course,

00:21:37.600 --> 00:21:39.010
the Pixel--

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:44.590
we've also launched the beta
program for seven other OEMs.

00:21:44.590 --> 00:21:47.350
And the reason why that's
been possible this time

00:21:47.350 --> 00:21:50.320
is because of investments
we made in Oreo

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:52.900
where we introduced a project
called Project Treble.

00:21:52.900 --> 00:21:55.010
You might have heard of that.

00:21:55.010 --> 00:21:56.860
It was talked about
a lot in the press

00:21:56.860 --> 00:21:59.840
when we launched
at IO last year.

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:03.100
But what I introduced is a
hardware abstraction layer

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:04.990
between all of
the device drivers

00:22:04.990 --> 00:22:07.940
and the kernel, versus
the rest of the OS

00:22:07.940 --> 00:22:10.600
or the system libraries,
the UI, et cetera.

00:22:10.600 --> 00:22:14.140
And what that's meant
is it's much simpler

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:19.030
for the OEMs, the
device manufacturers

00:22:19.030 --> 00:22:22.330
to update the operating
system on top of that existing

00:22:22.330 --> 00:22:25.700
bought support package
for the lower level code.

00:22:25.700 --> 00:22:29.950
And so now is a really,
really good opportunity

00:22:29.950 --> 00:22:32.470
to get devices into
your labs to start

00:22:32.470 --> 00:22:36.100
testing, really, the latest
functionality, because it's

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:39.250
coming down the pike,
actually, quite a lot quicker

00:22:39.250 --> 00:22:42.290
than previous releases.

00:22:42.290 --> 00:22:43.790
My colleague James
is actually going

00:22:43.790 --> 00:22:47.360
to be talking in a lot more
detail with some demos,

00:22:47.360 --> 00:22:49.850
as well, on the new
features in Android P.

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:53.240
So if you're interested in
that, do check that out at 3:15

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:54.800
over in the Metreon.

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:01.430
Let's touch a little
bit on deployment.

00:23:01.430 --> 00:23:04.770
So management's all
very well and good,

00:23:04.770 --> 00:23:08.470
but unless you can simply
deploy those devices

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:12.670
into an enterprise, you're still
going to have some challenges.

00:23:12.670 --> 00:23:14.830
Android has actually got
a choice of different ways

00:23:14.830 --> 00:23:16.914
that you can
bootstrap [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:16.914 --> 00:23:19.330
and this is particularly focused
on the fully managed kind

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:21.210
of devices.

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:26.010
You can use QR codes or NFC,
for NFC-compatible devices.

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:30.180
You can have a text or token
that's issued by your EMM.

00:23:30.180 --> 00:23:32.820
But the most recent
edition, which we introduced

00:23:32.820 --> 00:23:36.420
in Android Oreo, is zero touch.

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:39.600
So let's just talk a little
bit about the details

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:41.370
of zero touch.

00:23:41.370 --> 00:23:45.030
What this does is it gives you
a completely seamless experience

00:23:45.030 --> 00:23:47.460
when unboxing a device.

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:52.290
So you don't have to send
devices down a white glove

00:23:52.290 --> 00:23:54.690
service, or have them
pre-enrolled by an IT

00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:56.130
administrator.

00:23:56.130 --> 00:23:58.710
As soon as a user
unboxes that device,

00:23:58.710 --> 00:24:01.200
powers it on, and
connects it to a network--

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:02.760
and if it's a
smartphone, you might

00:24:02.760 --> 00:24:06.810
have a Sim in there, which will
just get a network anyway--

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:10.140
then it will automatically
read its configuration

00:24:10.140 --> 00:24:15.240
and its enrollment
details from a server

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:18.330
and then immediately download
that managing app, whichever

00:24:18.330 --> 00:24:23.850
app it is, for whichever
of your favorite EMM is,

00:24:23.850 --> 00:24:26.010
and then it will apply
the policy immediately.

00:24:26.010 --> 00:24:28.150
So you're up and
running, really,

00:24:28.150 --> 00:24:32.250
in very little time with very
little opportunity for the user

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:34.870
to make mistakes as well.

00:24:34.870 --> 00:24:36.840
The other cool
thing about this is

00:24:36.840 --> 00:24:39.870
if you were to try and set up
the device without connecting

00:24:39.870 --> 00:24:44.980
to a network, of course,
it can't talk to the server

00:24:44.980 --> 00:24:48.960
to get its policy, but as soon
as that device does connect

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:51.770
to a network, it goes ah ha.

00:24:51.770 --> 00:24:54.330
I should have been involved
in full management,

00:24:54.330 --> 00:24:56.690
and forced the user
to factory reset it,

00:24:56.690 --> 00:24:58.850
and then go into an
enrollment cycle.

00:24:58.850 --> 00:25:02.240
So it's also a little bit of
a kind of theft protection

00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:07.220
or protection against
misuse as well.

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:09.240
So just a little bit more
about how that works.

00:25:09.240 --> 00:25:11.650
Well, we need to get
cooperation from a number

00:25:11.650 --> 00:25:13.576
of different stakeholders.

00:25:13.576 --> 00:25:14.950
Now remember here,
we're not just

00:25:14.950 --> 00:25:17.770
doing this for one
device manufacturer.

00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:20.350
We're doing it for a
whole ecosystem, that

00:25:20.350 --> 00:25:22.580
has quite a few challenges.

00:25:22.580 --> 00:25:24.940
So first of all, we
need manufacturers

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:28.810
to certify their
solutions with us

00:25:28.810 --> 00:25:32.920
and also ensure that they are
putting unique serial numbers

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:34.630
onto devices.

00:25:34.630 --> 00:25:37.310
That's actually not always
happened in the past.

00:25:37.310 --> 00:25:42.490
So there's some process
assurance that needs to happen.

00:25:42.490 --> 00:25:44.490
We work with carriers
and resellers

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:46.720
as sort of the trusted
channel partner

00:25:46.720 --> 00:25:49.980
to ensure that when
devices are sold,

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:53.590
they're assigned to the correct
customer within Google's

00:25:53.590 --> 00:25:56.350
back end systems to
enforce that enrollment.

00:25:56.350 --> 00:26:00.550
Because what we want to avoid
is any sort of device hijacking

00:26:00.550 --> 00:26:03.160
after a device is sold.

00:26:03.160 --> 00:26:05.200
So we ensure that there.

00:26:05.200 --> 00:26:08.830
And then from a
customer perspective,

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:13.240
they can use their EMM then
to apply the correct policy

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:17.380
to that device so that when it
boots into an enrolled state,

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:21.820
it's straight in with
the right settings.

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:24.640
So we've already been
working extensively

00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:28.130
with partners in
all of those areas.

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:31.060
So this gives you a sense of
all of those different partners

00:26:31.060 --> 00:26:34.690
that support zero touch today.

00:26:34.690 --> 00:26:36.550
And we've got more coming soon.

00:26:36.550 --> 00:26:39.970
On the manufacturers, there
were a lot of the more dedicated

00:26:39.970 --> 00:26:43.000
type devices from companies
like Panasonic, Honeywell,

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:47.830
and Zebra, and there's more EMMs
and carriers in the pipe as,

00:26:47.830 --> 00:26:50.930
well, which unfortunately
I can't disclose today.

00:26:50.930 --> 00:26:54.460
But you'll see not
just region-specific,

00:26:54.460 --> 00:26:59.170
but organizations from around
the world who are participating

00:26:59.170 --> 00:27:00.020
in this program.

00:27:00.020 --> 00:27:05.450
So we're super excited with the
velocity that we've got there.

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:08.960
I want to switch gears now
to talk about app management.

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:12.950
And what we do with app
management in Android is,

00:27:12.950 --> 00:27:15.380
actually, we
leverage Google Play

00:27:15.380 --> 00:27:18.380
and then add our
management layer on top

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:21.860
to make sure that IT departments
have full control of what's

00:27:21.860 --> 00:27:23.360
going on.

00:27:23.360 --> 00:27:25.520
Now the question you're
all saying is, well,

00:27:25.520 --> 00:27:27.830
why would I want to
use Google Play, which

00:27:27.830 --> 00:27:30.230
is full of games
and lots of stuff,

00:27:30.230 --> 00:27:32.780
for managing enterprise apps?

00:27:32.780 --> 00:27:35.180
Well the great thing
about Google Play

00:27:35.180 --> 00:27:38.910
is it's a huge platform.

00:27:38.910 --> 00:27:40.200
It's secure.

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:43.920
It sort of authenticates
the install of these apps

00:27:43.920 --> 00:27:47.280
and it makes sure the apps
are appropriately signed.

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:50.460
It has global server
footprint, so we can always

00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:53.370
deliver the best
experience to your users

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:55.860
in terms of download
performance,

00:27:55.860 --> 00:27:58.930
wherever they are in the world.

00:27:58.930 --> 00:28:01.030
It applies patch updates.

00:28:01.030 --> 00:28:03.990
So you don't have to download
the same APK over and over

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:05.230
again.

00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:09.610
And we also do APK scanning,
which is, of course,

00:28:09.610 --> 00:28:11.550
part of our security model.

00:28:11.550 --> 00:28:14.000
But if you're thinking
about private apps

00:28:14.000 --> 00:28:16.890
or internally developed
apps, well, why would

00:28:16.890 --> 00:28:18.990
you want your APKs scanned?

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:21.450
Well, the interesting
thing about private apps

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:25.090
is that very often you're
using third party SDKs.

00:28:25.090 --> 00:28:27.750
You might be employing an
agency to build it for you

00:28:27.750 --> 00:28:31.920
and you've got no idea what
coding standards they uphold.

00:28:31.920 --> 00:28:36.210
So APK scanning not only
scans for bad things

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:37.350
happening around malware.

00:28:37.350 --> 00:28:42.360
But also against vulnerable
third party SDKs.

00:28:42.360 --> 00:28:48.040
So avoid things like Heartbleed
in OpenSSL, for example.

00:28:48.040 --> 00:28:50.650
It also comes with comprehensive
publishing tools, which

00:28:50.650 --> 00:28:53.770
you can use for your private
apps, crash and performance

00:28:53.770 --> 00:28:59.500
supporting, your other reporting
tools in terms of installs,

00:28:59.500 --> 00:29:02.350
it manages updates for
you, and then recently

00:29:02.350 --> 00:29:06.340
we just announced at Google IO
this concept of an Android app

00:29:06.340 --> 00:29:10.160
bundle where for
different devices,

00:29:10.160 --> 00:29:11.830
you might download
different assets.

00:29:11.830 --> 00:29:16.600
So let's say you've got
different image densities,

00:29:16.600 --> 00:29:20.940
different resolutions of
images assets within your APK.

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:24.010
But what Android App Bundle
does is it selectively

00:29:24.010 --> 00:29:26.440
installs just the
assets that are required

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:28.510
for the device that's
being installed,

00:29:28.510 --> 00:29:30.060
rather than everything.

00:29:30.060 --> 00:29:33.250
And that saves you bandwidth,
it saves you battery life,

00:29:33.250 --> 00:29:36.050
and it saves you
storage on the device.

00:29:36.050 --> 00:29:41.200
So lots of reasons why Google
Play is a great platform

00:29:41.200 --> 00:29:43.520
to build on top of.

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:46.040
And then bring the
management on top of that.

00:29:46.040 --> 00:29:49.040
You can then curate
an enterprise store

00:29:49.040 --> 00:29:53.750
using, of course, a familiar
user experience for your users.

00:29:53.750 --> 00:29:57.600
You can silently
push install apps.

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:00.720
So for example, when
a device is enrolled,

00:30:00.720 --> 00:30:03.530
you can push apps
automatically, as soon as

00:30:03.530 --> 00:30:06.350
that device boots
up, so that a user is

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:09.990
ready to work immediately.

00:30:09.990 --> 00:30:13.580
You can also push
configurations to those apps.

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:19.870
So for example, a VPN app can
be quite complicated to set up.

00:30:19.870 --> 00:30:23.360
There's lots of different, quite
technical parameters there.

00:30:23.360 --> 00:30:25.100
But you can make
sure a user doesn't

00:30:25.100 --> 00:30:27.470
have to worry about that by
sending the configuration

00:30:27.470 --> 00:30:30.820
bundle down to the app at
the same time as the install.

00:30:33.370 --> 00:30:35.140
I already commented
on the ability

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:37.180
to distribute private apps.

00:30:37.180 --> 00:30:39.610
We make sure that
those are logically

00:30:39.610 --> 00:30:43.150
separate from any
public apps on Play,

00:30:43.150 --> 00:30:45.910
so you can be confident that
they are only distributable

00:30:45.910 --> 00:30:47.620
to your employees.

00:30:47.620 --> 00:30:51.120
But you can also arrange them
within your enterprise app

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:54.430
store side by side
with public apps,

00:30:54.430 --> 00:30:56.320
so an employee
doesn't really have

00:30:56.320 --> 00:30:58.960
to worry about whether this is
a private app or a public app.

00:30:58.960 --> 00:31:03.030
They just install the apps
that are authorized to them.

00:31:03.030 --> 00:31:06.450
And then finally, the
new thing that we've

00:31:06.450 --> 00:31:10.890
introduced very recently is
a data processing agreement

00:31:10.890 --> 00:31:14.270
as part of our contractual
terms for Managed Google Play.

00:31:14.270 --> 00:31:20.430
What that means is that we've
got a contract in place to give

00:31:20.430 --> 00:31:24.240
you confidence that we will only
use any data that we collect

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:26.360
for providing the
managed play service,

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:27.810
and not for nothing else.

00:31:31.390 --> 00:31:33.220
Recently we've made
some improvements

00:31:33.220 --> 00:31:35.050
to Managed Play, of course.

00:31:35.050 --> 00:31:38.260
It's now possible to
programmatically publish

00:31:38.260 --> 00:31:40.510
private apps, rather
than having to go

00:31:40.510 --> 00:31:45.100
into a separate console, which
is the play publishing console.

00:31:45.100 --> 00:31:48.430
So there is a short link there
if you want to look at that.

00:31:48.430 --> 00:31:50.680
If you want to build that
into your own publishing

00:31:50.680 --> 00:31:52.780
tools, for example,
or your development

00:31:52.780 --> 00:31:55.690
workflows internally.

00:31:55.690 --> 00:32:00.250
There's a new API now to
determine how and when

00:32:00.250 --> 00:32:01.950
app updates happen.

00:32:01.950 --> 00:32:04.180
So whether you allow
them only over Wi-Fi

00:32:04.180 --> 00:32:09.130
or you'll also permit them
over cellular, or not at all.

00:32:09.130 --> 00:32:13.430
And you can also do per
device app authorization.

00:32:13.430 --> 00:32:16.530
So historically, that was
always done on a user basis.

00:32:16.530 --> 00:32:19.820
But if the same user
has a BYO smartphone

00:32:19.820 --> 00:32:21.770
and a corporate-owned
tablet, you

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:23.330
might actually
want to offer them

00:32:23.330 --> 00:32:25.880
different sets of apps
for that same user

00:32:25.880 --> 00:32:28.040
for different
security environments.

00:32:28.040 --> 00:32:31.090
And that's now possible.

00:32:31.090 --> 00:32:34.930
We're also introducing some
additional UI features.

00:32:34.930 --> 00:32:40.080
So this is an embeddable
widget that your EMMs will

00:32:40.080 --> 00:32:44.000
be able to embed and
you'd be pleased to know,

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:49.080
if you're using Google Mobile
Management as part of G Suite,

00:32:49.080 --> 00:32:52.110
these features are going to come
in as soon as they launch later

00:32:52.110 --> 00:32:53.550
on this quarter.

00:32:53.550 --> 00:32:55.680
So there'll be a
visual user interface

00:32:55.680 --> 00:32:58.470
for publishing
private apps, so it's

00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:03.090
part of the same EMM experience
you no longer have to chop

00:33:03.090 --> 00:33:05.100
and change between consoles.

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:07.980
We're introducing the concept
of web app publishing.

00:33:07.980 --> 00:33:14.610
So you'll be able to push a
web app to the user's launcher

00:33:14.610 --> 00:33:19.350
so that they can go straight to
the internet or other web tools

00:33:19.350 --> 00:33:21.180
that you might have.

00:33:21.180 --> 00:33:25.050
And they're also building a sort
of drag and drop store builder,

00:33:25.050 --> 00:33:29.020
so when you're curating
an enterprise app store,

00:33:29.020 --> 00:33:31.740
you can actually create
your clusters of apps.

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:36.090
You can place the apps where you
want in a very visual way, drag

00:33:36.090 --> 00:33:40.022
and drop, to make it very, very
quick to curate that enterprise

00:33:40.022 --> 00:33:41.730
app store and get that
out to your users.

00:33:44.560 --> 00:33:46.830
Finally, I want to talk
about the Android Management

00:33:46.830 --> 00:33:53.250
API, which is a new API that
we've built that really enables

00:33:53.250 --> 00:33:56.920
bespoke solutions
within an enterprise,

00:33:56.920 --> 00:33:59.910
or indeed, developers who want
to build device management

00:33:59.910 --> 00:34:01.840
into a wider solution.

00:34:01.840 --> 00:34:03.900
So maybe you're
building, I don't know,

00:34:03.900 --> 00:34:07.470
a meeting room
management product.

00:34:07.470 --> 00:34:10.679
But you want to manage those
tablets within the meeting room

00:34:10.679 --> 00:34:12.470
as part of the same product.

00:34:12.470 --> 00:34:13.970
And actually, Android
Management API

00:34:13.970 --> 00:34:18.389
is the best way now
to start doing it.

00:34:18.389 --> 00:34:20.969
It's a cloud API, so
rather than having

00:34:20.969 --> 00:34:24.070
to build an agent
app on the device,

00:34:24.070 --> 00:34:28.199
you can just send
policies over that API.

00:34:28.199 --> 00:34:34.320
And so it's much, much simpler
to have a single consistent API

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:39.360
rather than having to build
a agent, talk to Managed Play

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:44.070
separately, send push
notifications separately as

00:34:44.070 --> 00:34:45.179
well.

00:34:45.179 --> 00:34:46.870
Very, very powerful.

00:34:46.870 --> 00:34:49.080
We're updating that
every six weeks.

00:34:49.080 --> 00:34:51.630
We have a comprehensive
set of functionality.

00:34:51.630 --> 00:34:56.560
Not quite everything that
Android OS offers directly yet,

00:34:56.560 --> 00:34:58.620
but we're getting there.

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:02.460
And it's built by the same
team that builds the OS.

00:35:02.460 --> 00:35:06.810
So we make sure that we're
implementing the features

00:35:06.810 --> 00:35:09.690
exactly the way as
they were designed.

00:35:09.690 --> 00:35:12.660
And it's policy-based, so you
don't need to worry about all

00:35:12.660 --> 00:35:15.240
of the different
sequence of interactions.

00:35:15.240 --> 00:35:19.519
You can set a policy and
then just forget about it.

00:35:19.519 --> 00:35:21.810
So I'm actually going to be
talking in much more detail

00:35:21.810 --> 00:35:23.185
about that, if
you're interested.

00:35:23.185 --> 00:35:27.910
It'll be here tomorrow
afternoon at 4:35.

00:35:27.910 --> 00:35:32.170
OK, let's quickly
touch on security.

00:35:32.170 --> 00:35:35.920
And Android really takes a
defense in depth approach

00:35:35.920 --> 00:35:37.890
to security.

00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:40.540
So let's start at the bottom.

00:35:40.540 --> 00:35:45.410
On the hardware side, we support
dedicated security hardware,

00:35:45.410 --> 00:35:48.820
whether that's a trusted
execution environment as part

00:35:48.820 --> 00:35:56.320
of the standard chipset,
which is usually a dedicated

00:35:56.320 --> 00:36:01.110
part of the processor
onboard, or indeed

00:36:01.110 --> 00:36:03.430
discrete secure
element hardware,

00:36:03.430 --> 00:36:08.590
which adds extra tamper
resistance to the product.

00:36:08.590 --> 00:36:11.870
And some of the tasks
that can achieve

00:36:11.870 --> 00:36:14.450
are, you're first of all
managing authentication

00:36:14.450 --> 00:36:20.150
for things like key lock,
managing your encryption keys,

00:36:20.150 --> 00:36:24.540
and other very sensitive tasks.

00:36:24.540 --> 00:36:30.090
The OS itself is hardened
against potential attack.

00:36:30.090 --> 00:36:33.060
So we employ a number of
different technologies

00:36:33.060 --> 00:36:34.500
to ensure that.

00:36:34.500 --> 00:36:38.190
Things like address
randomisation

00:36:38.190 --> 00:36:43.380
to make sure that if a hacker
achieves a particular exploit,

00:36:43.380 --> 00:36:45.870
it's much more difficult
to replicate that from one

00:36:45.870 --> 00:36:48.470
device to the next.

00:36:48.470 --> 00:36:50.180
We, of course, have
the Management APIs,

00:36:50.180 --> 00:36:52.220
which can enforce your
management security

00:36:52.220 --> 00:36:56.090
policies over the top, and
then Google Play Protect,

00:36:56.090 --> 00:37:01.700
which is a always on,
call it a anti-virus

00:37:01.700 --> 00:37:04.760
or an anti-malware
product, which always

00:37:04.760 --> 00:37:10.040
is adapting to identify
and remove the latest

00:37:10.040 --> 00:37:11.990
malware from devices.

00:37:11.990 --> 00:37:15.710
So really keeping end
to end protection.

00:37:15.710 --> 00:37:17.930
I'm not going to go into
any more detail on security

00:37:17.930 --> 00:37:20.310
at this point.

00:37:20.310 --> 00:37:23.910
Dave Kleidermacher, who's
the VP of Android security

00:37:23.910 --> 00:37:27.980
is going to be presenting
there shortly this afternoon.

00:37:27.980 --> 00:37:32.190
So if you're interested in
that, do check that out.

00:37:32.190 --> 00:37:36.480
Finally, I want to close with
just the level of innovation

00:37:36.480 --> 00:37:40.470
that you benefit from when
you invest in Android.

00:37:40.470 --> 00:37:43.620
So this is not necessarily
specific enterprise features,

00:37:43.620 --> 00:37:45.840
but many, many different
things going on.

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:48.410
I just want to call out a few.

00:37:48.410 --> 00:37:51.450
So I've already touched
on augmented reality,

00:37:51.450 --> 00:37:55.330
but if you want to build
some of these cutting edge

00:37:55.330 --> 00:37:59.880
experiences, whether that's for
training or for field services,

00:37:59.880 --> 00:38:02.920
ARCore is a really
good place to start.

00:38:02.920 --> 00:38:05.320
ARCore is a library
which does some

00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:08.290
of the heavy lifting for you
so you can focus on, really,

00:38:08.290 --> 00:38:11.300
the value add
elements of your app.

00:38:11.300 --> 00:38:14.560
So things like motion
tracking, detecting

00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:17.890
surfaces, which you can then
place objects on virtually,

00:38:17.890 --> 00:38:20.680
or light estimation so
you can really make sure

00:38:20.680 --> 00:38:24.190
that your objects are
rendered appropriately

00:38:24.190 --> 00:38:26.170
for the environment
that you're looking at.

00:38:26.170 --> 00:38:28.630
All of that is
taken care for you

00:38:28.630 --> 00:38:33.430
so you don't have to worry
about the details, challenges

00:38:33.430 --> 00:38:36.630
with some of the sensors.

00:38:36.630 --> 00:38:40.770
Machine learning, obviously
a huge topic here at Next

00:38:40.770 --> 00:38:41.940
this week.

00:38:41.940 --> 00:38:45.540
But Android supports
a whole stack

00:38:45.540 --> 00:38:47.470
of different capabilities.

00:38:47.470 --> 00:38:49.920
So at the bottom
of the stack here,

00:38:49.920 --> 00:38:51.970
you've got the
Neural Networks API.

00:38:51.970 --> 00:38:54.900
So that's a native
API on Android

00:38:54.900 --> 00:38:56.550
that really helps
you figure out which

00:38:56.550 --> 00:38:59.670
is the most optimal hardware
to use for your inference

00:38:59.670 --> 00:39:02.220
and for your learning.

00:39:02.220 --> 00:39:05.120
So if all you've got is a CPU,
then it will default to that,

00:39:05.120 --> 00:39:07.910
all the way up to some
of the latest devices

00:39:07.910 --> 00:39:10.830
have TPUs built into them.

00:39:10.830 --> 00:39:12.950
And of course,
Neural Networks API

00:39:12.950 --> 00:39:17.780
will make sure that those
fastest hardware is used.

00:39:17.780 --> 00:39:20.570
TensorFlow Lite then
abstracts that a bit further,

00:39:20.570 --> 00:39:25.080
and you can build neural
nets very quickly,

00:39:25.080 --> 00:39:27.120
with just a few lines of code.

00:39:27.120 --> 00:39:29.490
All the way up to ML
Kit, which enables

00:39:29.490 --> 00:39:31.770
you to do your
learning in the cloud,

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:34.140
compress that neural
network model,

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:37.439
and then deploy it to
devices for inference very,

00:39:37.439 --> 00:39:37.980
very quickly.

00:39:40.630 --> 00:39:44.650
Devices like here, the
Huawei P20 have already

00:39:44.650 --> 00:39:47.770
got that TPU chip
built in, and here

00:39:47.770 --> 00:39:51.010
you can see a performance
improvement of 10x.

00:39:51.010 --> 00:39:55.810
So a tenth of the time
taken to do a neural network

00:39:55.810 --> 00:39:57.940
inference using this product.

00:39:57.940 --> 00:40:00.820
So there are really
huge advancements

00:40:00.820 --> 00:40:06.340
taking place across the
Android ecosystem there.

00:40:06.340 --> 00:40:08.660
And then another thing,
I already mentioned IoT,

00:40:08.660 --> 00:40:12.900
but Android Things went
[INAUDIBLE] earlier this year,

00:40:12.900 --> 00:40:18.210
and a huge opportunity
for enterprises

00:40:18.210 --> 00:40:21.840
to leverage the same
technologies that we've built,

00:40:21.840 --> 00:40:24.550
whether that's from
the security side,

00:40:24.550 --> 00:40:28.080
so making sure you've got things
that hardware root of trust,

00:40:28.080 --> 00:40:31.320
and all the encryption key
support, all the way up

00:40:31.320 --> 00:40:35.280
to the connectivity options
and the management support

00:40:35.280 --> 00:40:37.790
that you need.

00:40:37.790 --> 00:40:40.990
So just then to
conclude, I really

00:40:40.990 --> 00:40:44.500
encourage you, when you're
thinking about mobile devices

00:40:44.500 --> 00:40:48.640
and endpoints in general, think
beyond the knowledge worker

00:40:48.640 --> 00:40:52.750
situation or scenario of, like,
devices in people's pockets,

00:40:52.750 --> 00:40:55.720
and think about what are
the opportunities you have

00:40:55.720 --> 00:40:58.900
to improve your
employee workflows

00:40:58.900 --> 00:41:01.060
or even end customer
experiences,

00:41:01.060 --> 00:41:06.230
whether that's in a
retail store or elsewhere.

00:41:06.230 --> 00:41:09.620
Take a look at the Android
Enterprise Recommended program.

00:41:09.620 --> 00:41:12.440
If you are in the
process or soon

00:41:12.440 --> 00:41:15.950
to be looking at what
devices to purchase,

00:41:15.950 --> 00:41:17.940
Android Enterprise
Recommended can give you

00:41:17.940 --> 00:41:21.350
a really good sense of
what are the devices that

00:41:21.350 --> 00:41:25.700
meet some of those critical
enterprise requirements,

00:41:25.700 --> 00:41:27.830
and also what are those--

00:41:27.830 --> 00:41:32.240
could you leverage that for
curating the set of devices

00:41:32.240 --> 00:41:34.290
that you support within a BYOD.

00:41:37.070 --> 00:41:40.460
And then finally, when you're
purchasing your devices,

00:41:40.460 --> 00:41:43.780
make sure that you're doing that
through a zero touch enabled

00:41:43.780 --> 00:41:47.150
reseller, because as
you're purchasing them,

00:41:47.150 --> 00:41:50.690
then that reseller will be able
to mark those in the zero touch

00:41:50.690 --> 00:41:53.450
system that are owned
by you, and then

00:41:53.450 --> 00:41:56.690
you can benefit from that
really frictionless approach

00:41:56.690 --> 00:42:03.760
to enrolling devices when
they're deployed to users.

00:42:03.760 --> 00:42:07.150
And then finally, take
a look at Android P.

00:42:07.150 --> 00:42:10.270
There's now eight different
device manufacturers

00:42:10.270 --> 00:42:13.060
that support that, so
really great opportunity now

00:42:13.060 --> 00:42:18.040
to get that into your test labs
and see what the opportunities

00:42:18.040 --> 00:42:20.020
are that that brings.

00:42:20.020 --> 00:42:22.855
So with that, just
to close, here's

00:42:22.855 --> 00:42:25.720
a quick summary of the other
sessions going on about Android

00:42:25.720 --> 00:42:27.280
Enterprise this week.

00:42:27.280 --> 00:42:30.130
Feel free to take a photo.

00:42:30.130 --> 00:42:32.890
The only one I haven't
mentioned is the last one,

00:42:32.890 --> 00:42:36.790
using mobile as a catalyst
for digital transformation.

00:42:36.790 --> 00:42:39.140
My colleague Sean-- sorry,
I will get out of the way,

00:42:39.140 --> 00:42:40.630
then you can take a photo--

00:42:40.630 --> 00:42:44.410
my colleague Sean is going to be
talking through a bit more high

00:42:44.410 --> 00:42:47.470
level around like
how can you apply

00:42:47.470 --> 00:42:51.860
Android and its technologies
to really transform businesses.

00:42:51.860 --> 00:42:53.590
Thank you very much.

00:42:53.590 --> 00:42:56.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

